<title>UK: UK unit wage costs up 4.5% in 3m to Aug.</title>
<headline>UK unit wage costs up 4.5% in 3m to Aug.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-10-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Office for National Statistics (ONS) released the following data on unit wage costs in UK manufacturing. (previously announced data in brackets)</p>
<p>    UNIT WAGE COSTS	    AUGUST     JULY   (PREL JULY)</p>
<p>    Monthly change vs yr ago 5.4	  3.9	(3.5)</p>
<p>    Index (base=1990)	  114.4	114.0   (113.5)</p>
<p>    3-mth versus same yr/ago 4.5*	 4.0	(3.7)</p>
<p>    Index (base=1990)	  114.0	113.3  (113.1)</p>
<p>PRODUCTIVITY</p>
<p>    monthly change vs yr ago -0.9	 0.4	(0.4)</p>
<p>    Index (base=1990)	  120.8	121.2  (121.2)</p>
<p>    3mth vs same year ago    -0.1	 0.3	(0.3)</p>
<p>    Index (base=1990)	  121.0	121.2  (121.3)</p>
<p>* Highest since October 1991.</p>
<p>The ONS said the results from the annual employment series changed the profile of the productivity series. As a result, unit wage cost and productivity figures were delayed.</p>
<p>The 0.1 percent fall in productivity over the three months to August was the first fall since December 1995.</p>
</text>
